Hello Jonathan,

A couple of things I noticed.

When using the transpose function, the dataframe you put in will become a matrix and matrices can contain only one type of data and if you have even a single non numeric value (like date) the whole thing becomes a ‘character-filled’ matrix (or otherwise non-numeric matrix). I would suggest sub setting the data prior to transposing:

occu.tmp <- read.csv("Occupancy.csv")

Ymat.tmp <- occu.tmp[,2:6]
Ymat <- t (occu.tmp)

# now the covs, which you want to be a dataframe
Cov.tmp <- occu.tmp[,7:10]
Cov <- as.data.frame (t (Cov.tmp))

library(unmarked)

umf <- unmarkedFrameOccu(y=Ymat, obsCovs=Cov)

This should have things in the format required but, I notice that you were looking to transpose to turn a site x visit matrix to a visit x site matrix. I dont see that you are doing this here because you have 5 'site' which aren’t site as far as I can see, they are species?

Perhaps I missed something?

Anyway,  how the other stuff is useful.

Chris Sutherland PhD

Postdoctoral Research Associate

Department of Natural Resources

New York Cooperative Fish and Wildlife Research Unit

Cornell University

