>
> Take this example y data (assuming no secondary sampling periods for simplicity):
>
>       y1 y2 y3 y4 y5 ... yN
> site1  1  3 NA  4  3 ...  5
>
> Now take this example yearlySiteCov data (none missing) of a generic variable (displayed in a wide format here):
>
>       y1 y2 y3 y4 y5 ... yN
> site1 10 11  9 10  8 ...  8
>
> I learned from an earlier response (likely from Richard) that yearlySiteCovs are relative to the period following the year, such that the change in site1 between year 1 and 2 is predicted by the yearlySiteCov data in year 1 (10).
>
> My question is how (and if) yearlySiteCovs predict changes in counts when y data is missing?  For example, as y data is missing from year 3, does pcountOpen use the yearlySiteCov data for year 2 (11), year 3 (9), both year 2 and 3 (somehow), or none in predicting changes in y between year 2 and year 4?  This example is for one missing year, but is the answer different when y data is missing for two (or more) consecutive years?
